00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.710 align:start position:0%
first session of iraq's newly elected  parliament began with the ceremony but  quickly turned it angry its first job  was picking a speaker  two competing shia blocks the southwest  movement and an iranian-backed coalition  each claimed to have a parliamentary  majority at one point the debate became  so heated that the assembly's temporary  leader mahmoud al-mashadhani had to  leave the chamber to seek medical  attention eventually  the former speaker muhammad al-halbosi  was re-elected along with deputy  speakers from the southwest movement and  the kurdistan democratic party  but the day's events showed that little  has changed  since last october's elections  the way political blocs enter the  parliament with their old rivalries and  ideologies and practices in mind  indicates a lack of harmony and reflects  a hostile message conveyed across  under iraq's post-saddam hussein  constitution the parliament now has 30  days to elect the country's new  president who will then ask the  assembly's largest block to form a  government the speaker says he wants to  do all this in just half that time but  choosing a prime minister will be  difficult because shia cleric mukta the  sodar and the iranian-backed parties  have such different agendas  and sunday's drama points toward an even  more difficult time when it comes to  picking a cabinet  the losing parties are expected to take  legal actions at the supreme court again  and deploy their supporters to the  streets but that will not change  anything  iraqis have waited for long to see their  new house of representatives in session  but some fear that real change may once  again be derailed by sectarian  differences  some iraqis see the newly elected  parliament as a chance to end corruption  and begin a new era  but in a landscape market by uncertainty  and political rivalry  it is not clear whether this new  legislature  is able or willing  to address those concerns  mahmoud
 

